 ## Overview
-Scanline Gradient Computation is a memory-efficient method for computing 3D gradient vectors and surface normals for scalar volume data by processing one scanline (a 1D row of voxels) at a time. It solves the problem of high memory bandwidth and storage requirements when computing derivatives for large volumes, which typically require accessing neighboring voxels in all three dimensions. It sets itself apart from batch processing methods like [VolPack Volume Normals Computation](3126-volpack-volume-normals-computation.md) by maintaining only a sliding window of three slices in memory, making it suitable for volumes that exceed available RAM.
+Scanline Gradient Computation is a memory-efficient method for computing 3D gradient vectors and surface normals for scalar volume data by processing one scanline at a time [S1]. It solves the problem of high memory bandwidth and storage requirements when computing derivatives for large volumes by maintaining only a sliding window of three slices in memory, making it suitable for volumes that exceed available RAM [S1]. It sets itself apart from batch processing methods like [VolPack Volume Normals Computation](3126-volpack-volume-normals-computation.md) by streaming data slice-by-slice rather than requiring the entire scalar array in memory [S1].
 
